Topic5: File Permissions:
-------------------------
Linux file permissions control who can read, write, or execute a file or directory
These permission are crucial for system security and user management

Types of permissions & Users
----------------------------
Three Types of permissions:

	.Read(r): View contents of a file/directory
	.Write(w): Modify file or add/remove in directory
	.Execute(x): Run file (script/program) or access directory

Three Types of Users:

	.Owner(user): who created the file/owner of the file
	.Group: User in files group
	.Others: All other users not a owner or not in group

Permission Format:
------------------	
use ls -l to see permission (E.g., -rwxrw-r--)

Breakdown: (-rwxrw-r--)

Symbol			Meaning
-				Regular file (d for directory)
rwx				Owner: read, write, Execute
rw-				Group: read, write, No permission
r--				Others: read, No permission, No permission

Numeri(Octal) Notation:
-----------------------
Each permission had a value:

	. r=4
	. w=2
	. x=1    Total value is '7'

To set permissions: "chmod 755 filename"

	Owner: rwx(7=4+2+1)
	Group: r-x(5=4+0+1)
	Others:r-x(5=4+0+1)
	
	Full permission: 777
	Zero permission: 000

Symbolic	Numeric	Permissions
,---	0	None
,--x	1	Execute
,-w-	2	Write
,-wx	3	Write+Execute
r--	4	Read
r-x	5	read+Execute
rw-	6	read+write
rwx	7	read+write+Execute

Changing Permission and Ownership:
---------------------------------
View permission: "ls -ls"
Change permission: "chmod [options] [permission] file"
Change Owner: chown user file


Topic:6
-------
$: This is normal user with limited permissions
#: Super user/root user with all permissions

To convert normal user to super/root user: "sudo su"

Topic:7 copy, move or rename files and directories
---------------------------------------------------
1.copy commands(cp)
	.cp raju shiva
	
2.copy file to directory
	.cp shiva /path/to/dir
	.cp shiva /home/ec2-user/nexgen
	
3.copy Multiple files
	.cp dinesh naresh raju /home/ec2-user/nexgen
	
4.Move a file
	.mv shiva /home/ec2-user/nexgen

5.Move a directory
	.mv nexgen /home/ec2-user/yash/priya

6.Rename commands(mv)
Linux does not have a separate rename command; mv is used for renaming

rename a file:
	.mv oldname newname
	
remove a directory(old)	

Topic:8 Reporting File system disk space usage
----------------------------------------------
Learn how to check disk and memory usage easily on linux

Disk space usage commands:
	.df -h : Report file system disk space usage (human readable)
	.du -h : Estimate file space usage(human readable)
	
Memory Usage Commands:
	.free : Display amount of free and used memory in the system
	.free -h : Human readable memory output
	.free -k : Display memory in kilobytes(KB)
	.free -m : Display memory usage in megabytes(MB)
	.free -g : Display memory usage in gigabytes(GB)

Topic:9 Package management:
---------------------------
.is used to install, update, configure and remove software.
.It ensures that software package and dependencies are handled properly and maintain(secure and manage their system efficently)	
	 
Example:
	
	.Web server -> serves websites (e.g., Apache, Nginx)
	.Database server -> stores and manages data (e.g., MySQL, Postgres, microsoft etc)
	.File Server -> stores and shares files between servers
	.Mail server -> handles emails (e.g., Microsoft Exchange)
	.Application Server -> runs business logics
	
EC2 server:
-----------	
	when you choose amazon Linux/debian/ubuntu/redhat/centos:
	
	Task:					Debian/Ubuntu:					Redhat/Amazon linux/CentOS:
	--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	Update					sudo apt update					sudo yum update
	upgrade all packages	sudo apt upgrade				sudo yum upgrade
	Install package			sudo apt install packagename	sudo yum install packagename
	remove package			sudo apt remove packagename		sudo yum remove packagename
	List installed packages	apt list --installed			yum list installed
	
Key Features & Functions:
-------------------------
	.Install: add new software
	.Upgrade: update installed packages
	.Remove: Uninstall software

Protocols:
----------
Inbound rules: 	ssh(secure shell used to connect linux, 22
				http(hypertext transport protocol, used to access unsecured websites, port 80
				https(hypertext transport protocol secure, used to access secured websites, port 443
				RDP; remote desk protocol only windows
				mysql
Outbound rules:All traffic


TOPIC:10 Service Management:
----------------------------
you can start, stop, restart and monitor services 

Key service managment commands:
-------------------------------
For Example:
	
Action:					Command Example:
----------------------------------------
start service			sudo systemctl start service
stop service			sudo systemctl stop service
restart service			sudo systemctl restart service

Examples:
	.Start Apache: sudo systemctl start nginx/apache/httpd
	.stop Apache: sudo systemctl stop httpd
	.restart Apache: sudo systemctl restart httpd
				

Topic:11 user management in Linux:
----------------------------------
.Manage users and groups using command-line tools
.Create, modify, view and delete users and groups
.groups nothing but collection of users

Creating users:
	.useradd/adduser: Adding new user (ex: useraddd shiva)
	 shiva:!!:20413:0:99999:7:::
	a)shiva: This is the username you created
	b)!!: password is locked or not set 
	C)20413: date when the password was last changed
	d)0:Minimum days between password changes
	e)99999: Maximum days before password expires
	f)7: Warining period before password expiry
	g)::: = inactivity or account expiry
	
Setting and managing passwords:
	.passwd: set or change user password (Ex: passwd shiva/kumar)

logging to account using username and password:
	.su username

To exit from the specific user:
 	.ctrl+d
	
To view all users:
	.cat /etc/shadow
	.cat /etc/passwd
		
Viewing user information:
	.id: show users UID and group IDs (Ex: id shiva)

Deleting Users:
	.userdel: delete user (Ex: userdel shiva/kumar)
	.deluser: Delete user and thier files (Ex: deluser shiva/kumar)


Group management:
-----------------
	.groupadd: Adding new group (ex: groupadd groupname)
	.cat /etc/group: checking group exist or not
	.usermod: Add user to a group (ex: usermod -aG groupname username)
	.groupdel: Delete group (ex: groupdel groupname)

Shell Scripting:
Bash shell scripting refers to writing series of commands for linux shell, known as the BASH SHELL
BASH = Bourne again shell
Bash used in a sequence to automate tasks or perform complex operations
Bash is defualt shell for linux for most like operating systems

Key Aspects of Bash shell scripting:
------------------------------------
1.Scripting Lanaguage: bash scripting uses the bash shell as an interpreter(#!/usr/bin/bash)
to execute commands written in a script file these script can be used for wide range of purpose, 
from simple file manupulation to more complex task like system admin or automation of repetitive
task.

2.Syntax: Bash script are written using shell commands, control structures (like loops, and conditions),
variables, functions, and comments.
the syntax is similar to other programming languages but toilored specially for interacting
with shell environment

3.Automation:
One of the primary uses of Bash scripting is automation.By writing scripts, admins and users
can automate routine task,
such as:
File backups
Log file analysis
software installations
system monitoring and more

Note:This automation saves the time and reduces human errors

4.#!/usr/bin/bash: shebang interpreter path, first line of the shell scripting
shell file extension is .sh

5.Shell file execution commands:
To Run or Execute shell file
a.sh filename.sh
b.bash filename.sh
c. ./filename.sh  (but should give execute permission to file)

what is Bash?
Shell is a program that accept commands from a user and communicates with the
operating system

Bash means: Bourne Again Shell

when you type:
pwd
ls
mkdir demo
you are interacting with a shell.

Check the current shell:
echo $SHELL
output: /bin/bash

check bash version:
bash --version

what is Bash scripting?
instead of manually executing 10 Linux commands every day, we can put those commands into a file
and execute them together
For Example:
mkdir backup
cp import.txt backup/
date
df -h

Save those commands inside a .sh file, and it becomes a simple Bash script

Note:
This is extremely useful in DevOps, Cloud admins, CI/CD, server management, monotoring, backups and
deployments

Hands-on 1 - First Bash Script
Create your working directory
mkdir bash-training
cd bash-training

nano hello.sh
#!/bin/bash

echo "Hello Students"
echo "Welcome to Linux Bash Scripting"
echo "Today we are learning automation"

save the file : ctrl+x, yes and enter

Run: bash hello.sh

3.Understanding #!/bin/bash
This "#!/bin/bash" is called shebang
it tells linux which interpreter should execute the script

#!/bin/bash --> Use Bash to execute this file

Hands-on 2 - Make Script Executable
check permissions: ls -l hello.sh

you can see there is no execute permission
add : chmod +x hello.sh
now execute: ./hello.sh

4.Comments:
Comments explain code and aren't executed.
# This is a comment
echo "Hello"

Example:
#!/bin/bash
# Author: Siva
# Purpose: Bash training demo
echo "Bash Training"

5.Variables:
Stores a value.

name="Siva"
course="Bash Scripting"

Wrong:
name = "Siva"
Correct:
name="siva"

Both does not permit spaces around=in variable assignment

Access a variable using $:
echo $name

prefer braces when embedding variables:
echo "${name}"

Hands-on 3 - variables:
create: nano variables.sh
add:
#!/bin/bash

name="Siva"
course="Linux Bash Scripting"
duration="10 hours"

echo "Trainer: $name"
echo "Course: $course"
echo "Duration: $duration"

Run: chmod +x variables.sh
     ./variables.sh

6.User Input-read
static variables are hardcorded:
name="shiva"
Note: sometimes we want the user to provide the value

Use:
read name

Better:
read -p "Enter your name: " name

Hands-on 4 - Interactive Script
--------------------------------
nano student.sh

add:
#!/bin/bash
read -p "Enter your name: " name
read -p "Enter your course: " course

echo "-------------------------"
echo "Student Name : $name"
echo "Course 	   : $course"
echo "-------------------------"

Save file: ctrl+x, y and enter
Run: chmod +x student.sh
     ./student.sh
Enter your name: shiva
Coure          : Linux

7.Numbers and Arthimetic:
-------------------------
bash variables values are generally treated as strings unless you use an arithmetic
context.
Example:
a=10
b=20

calculate:
sum=$((a + b))

Then:
echo "$sum"

Common operators:
-----------------
Operator		Meaning
+				Addition
-				Substraction
*				Multiplication
/				division
%				Reminder

Hands-on 5 - calculator:
------------------------
nano calculator.sh
Add:
#!/bin/bash

read -p "Enter first number: " num1
read -p "Enter second number: " num2

sum=$((num1 + num2))
difference=$((num1 - num2))
product=$((num1 * num2))

echo "Addition : $sum"
echo "Substraction : $difference"
echo "Multiplication : $product"

Run:
chmod +x calculator.sh
./calulator.sh

Enter first number: 20
Enter second number: 10

Addition : 30
Substraction : 10
Multiplication : 200

8.Command Substitution:
-----------------------
This is one of the most useful Bash concepts for DevOPs

suppose you run: date
Instead of simply displaying it, we can capture its Output:
current_date=$(date)

Then:
echo "$current_date"

Syntax:
variable=$(command)
Examples:
hostname=$(hostname)
user=$(whoami)
kernel=$(uname -r)

Hands-on 6 - Server information Script:
----------------------------------------
create: nano server-info.sh
Add:
#!/bin/bash
hostname=$(hostname)
user=$(whoami)
kernel=$(uname -r)
current_date=$(date)
uptime_info=$(uptime -p)

echo "========================="
echo "     SERVER INFORMATION"
echo "========================="
echo "Hostname : $hostname"
echo "User : $user"
echo "Kernel : $kernel"
echo "Date : $current_date"
echo "Uptime : $uptime_info"
echo "======================="

Run:
chmod +x server-info.sh
./server-info.sh

This is your first practical devops

9.Special Variables
-------------------
Bash automatically provides special variables
Create : nano arguments.sh
Add:
#!/bin/bash

echo "Script Name : $0"
echo "First Argument : $1"
echo "Second Argument : $2"
echo "Total Argument : $#"
echo "All Arguments : $@"

Run:
chmod +x arguments.sh
./arguments.sh Shiva DevOps

Note: you should see values corresponding to the script and supplied arguments
Important variables:
--------------------
Variable		Meaning
$0				Script name
$1				First argument
$2				Second argument
$#				Number of arguments
$@				All arguments
$?				Exit status of previous command
$$				Current shell/process PID (Process Identifier)





















